1: # A period-equivariant lattice resolution relative to the actual boundaries
2: 
3: Working audit of the next local fan step for K39. Start with the three
4: actual sector fans and their common H refinement constructed in
5: `notes/part3_source_zero_prism_audit.md`, in its default negative-fan
6: marking. The actual E chain is prescribed. This argument constructs a
7: period-equivariant unimodular subdivision while retaining that chain
8: and the I boundary. It does not assert properness of the analytic
9: unit quotient or a completed-family return.
10: 
11: ## ER.1. Protect the actual regular boundary subfan
12: 
13: Protect the prescribed I boundary and the complete E chain, and also
14: protect the already regular W boundary. This extra protection changes
15: none of the requested data. The I cones are the unimodular
16: negative-diagonal lattice triangles at height one. Adjacent E rays
17: are \((0,j,1,0),(0,j+1,1,0)\), whose nonzero two-dimensional
18: minor is one in absolute value. Adjacent W rays have the form
19: \((i,-ai,1,0),(i+1,-a(i+1),1,0)\), with the same unit minor.
20: Their span lattices are saturated: in W the relation is
21: \(y=-ax\), and in E it is \(x=0\). Thus every cone in this
22: protected subfan is regular in its actual saturated lattice.
23: 
24: In each sector the protected subfan is precisely the chosen
25: subdivision on the base face of height \(s=0\). Every cone
26: outside it contains a ray with positive H height. On such a ray
27: \(p=(x,r,s)\), the equality \(T_m p=p\) implies
28: \[
29:                          (rP_0+sH)m=0.
30: \]
31: The explicit determinant polynomials PF.3 are positive for
32: \(r\ge0,s>0\). Hence this period stabilizer is trivial.
33: On either rank-one base face, its kernel stabilizer fixes that
34: entire face pointwise; no chosen resolution centre will lie there.
35: This accounts for those stabilizers without replacing their analytic
36: unit action by the identity.
37: 
38: ## ER.2. An exact separation subdivision
39: 
40: Take a relative barycentric subdivision outside the protected
41: subfan. Concretely process old faces in increasing dimension. On a
42: face outside the protected subfan insert the interior ray that is
43: the sum of its primitive ray generators, reduced to its primitive
44: integral vector, and cone its already subdivided boundary from that
45: ray. Leave protected faces unchanged. The convex height-one
46: cross-section gives the elementary radial proof of coverage and
47: common faces, just as in PF.4. The choices on the common H subfan
48: are the same in every sector, since its actual primitive generators
49: are already identified.
50: 
51: The resulting cones consist of a face of the protected subfan
52: together with a strictly increasing chain of nonprotected old
53: faces, represented by their inserted rays. The period maps are
54: unimodular and preserve face dimension and the protected subfan;
55: they carry each sum to the corresponding sum. The subdivision is
56: therefore exactly equivariant.
57: 
58: It has the following useful property:
59: 
60: **Each cone contains at most one ray from each nonprotected period
61: orbit.** Indeed two such rays in one orbit come from old faces of
62: the same dimension. A strictly increasing face chain contains only
63: one face of that dimension. The inserted ray is in the relative
64: interior of its named old face, so equal rays name equal faces.
65: Protected rays may repeat their orbit in a cone; this does not
66: affect the assertion.
67: 
68: Every singular face contains a nonprotected ray. A face generated
69: only by protected rays is contained in the protected base face of
70: its sector cone, and is a face of that regular simplicial boundary
71: cone. It is therefore regular. Each nonprotected ray has positive
72: H height and hence has trivial period stabilizer by ER.1.
73: 
74: Consequently two distinct translates of one singular face cannot
75: be faces of a single cone. Choose a nonprotected ray of that face;
76: the two translates would put two rays from its orbit in the same
77: cone. The separation property makes them equal, and triviality of
78: their stabilizer makes the translating element the identity. This
79: is the exact orbit separation needed for simultaneous star
80: subdivision; it does not rely on an unspecified sufficiently fine
81: metric subdivision.
82: 
83: ## ER.3. The integral lattice point and its literal star
84: 
85: Let C be a singular maximal cone, with primitive columns
86: \(r_1,\ldots,r_4\), matrix B, and determinant absolute value
87: \(D>1\). There is a nonzero integral point in its half-open
88: fundamental parallelepiped. Here is an explicit construction.
89: Some column \(B^{-1}e_j\) is nonintegral; otherwise \(B^{-1}\)
90: would be integral and its determinant would force \(D=1\).
91: Take its coordinatewise fractional part \(\alpha\), and put
92: \[
93:  p=B\alpha=e_j-B\lfloor B^{-1}e_j\rfloor,
94:               \qquad 0\le\alpha_i<1.                    \tag{ER.1}
95: \]
96: The vector p is integral and nonzero. Divide it by the greatest
97: common divisor of its coordinates to obtain its primitive generator;
98: divide all \(\alpha_i\) by that same positive integer. The
99: strict inequalities remain true. We use these primitive versions
100: in the sequel.
101: 
102: Let F be the face generated by those \(r_i\) with
103: \(\alpha_i>0\). The point p is in its relative interior.
104: The face F is singular: a regular face's primitive generators are
105: part of an integral basis of the saturated span, so an integral
106: combination with all coefficients in \([0,1)\) is zero.
107: That contradicts p. In particular F lies outside the protected
108: regular subfan and has a trivial period stabilizer.
109: 
110: Insert p on F, and insert \(T_m p\) on each translate
111: \(T_m F\). This is well defined. ER.2 proves that no old cone
112: contains two distinct such faces, so these simultaneous stars
113: cannot give competing subdivisions of one cone. On a cone C'
114: containing F, the new cones replace one \(r_i\) of F by p,
115: for each \(\alpha_i>0\). The coefficients on F are the same
116: ones as in (ER.1), because F has the same primitive generators
117: in every containing cone.
118: 
119: Coverage and common faces of this star are elementary. Write a
120: point as \(x=\sum t_i r_i\), \(t_i\ge0\). Subtract
121: \(\tau p\), where
122: \(\tau=\min_{\alpha_i>0}t_i/\alpha_i\). The remaining
123: coefficients are nonnegative and at least one is zero, placing x
124: in the corresponding new cone. Ties give exactly their common
125: faces. The same formula on shared old faces proves compatibility.
126: 
127: For every affected maximal cone C', determinant multilinearity gives
128: \[
129:              D(C'_i)=\alpha_i D(C'),\qquad
130:                     0<\alpha_i<1.                       \tag{ER.2}
131: \]
132: These are positive integers, since all new columns are integral.
133: Thus every affected determinant strictly decreases, including
134: maximal cones other than the initially selected one. No protected
135: boundary cone is subdivided: a singular F cannot be a face of a
136: regular protected cone, and the inserted point has positive H height.
137: 
138: ## ER.4. Separation survives and the algorithm terminates
139: 
140: The ray-orbit separation property survives each simultaneous star.
141: Every new cone is contained in an old cone. Old nonprotected ray
142: orbits retain the property. An old cone receives at most one new
143: ray from the newly chosen orbit, by ER.2. That orbit was not an
144: old ray orbit: its point lies in the relative interior of a face
145: of dimension at least two, so the old fan could not already
146: contain that ray. Every new nonprotected ray still has positive
147: H height and trivial stabilizer. Hence the same orbit-wise star
148: construction is available at the next stage, without another
149: separation subdivision.
150: 
151: The sector collection has finitely many cone orbits initially, and
152: the relative barycentric subdivision has finitely many face-chain
153: types. Each simultaneous star introduces finitely many new cone
154: orbits: each old cone receives at most one centre and at most four
155: replacement cones. Choose at every stage a maximal cone orbit
156: of largest determinant \(D_{\max}>1\). Its star removes at
157: least that orbit. Equation (ER.2) creates no orbit of determinant
158: \(D_{\max}\), and decreases every affected determinant.
159: Thus the ordered pair
160: \[
161:   (D_{\max},\ \text{number of maximal orbits with determinant}
162:                       \ D_{\max})                         \tag{ER.3}
163: \]
164: strictly decreases lexicographically. Both entries are finite
165: positive integers. There are finitely many stages at a given first
166: entry, and finitely many decreases of that entry. The construction
167: terminates with determinant one on every maximal cone; all its
168: faces are then regular as well.
169: 
170: Whenever the chosen F lies in the common H subfan, its ray and star
171: are applied in every incident sector. Their face generators agree,
172: so (ER.2) decreases each incident determinant and the subdivisions
173: agree on H. Count all three sectors in (ER.3). This proves one
174: compatible period-equivariant unimodular subdivision relative to
175: the exact I boundary, W boundary, and E chain.
176: 
177: The construction retains the original lattice throughout. It does
178: not declare a rational inspection coordinate to be integral, and
179: does not introduce an analytic filling on a rank-one base face.
180: All bounded period units, their contraction, and the source/cap
181: gluing remain the separate analytic obligations of K39.
182: 
183: ## Exact certificate
184: 
185: `code/part3/source_zero_equivariant_resolution.py` executes the
186: half-open-point construction, primitive division, minimal-face
187: singularity test, and every new determinant for the actual signed
188: prism cones at the first reduced and good root orders. It also
189: checks the protected boundary unit minors and the exact period
190: transport of a selected star. These computations accompany the
191: general finite-orbit and termination proof above; they are not
192: used as a bounded substitute for it.
